WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.950
- [Narrator] Inflation is dropping

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.070
and the Federal Reserve is
holding interest rates steady,

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.793
signaling the potential for.

00:00:06.793 --> 00:00:08.430
- A soft landing.
- Soft landing.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.870
- Is this the soft landing?

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.900
- A soft landing is
basically economic nirvana.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.860
They require the right
mix of policymaking skill

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.230
and just plain economic luck.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.990
- [Narrator] But we don't
see soft landings very often

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.730
because they're hard to achieve.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.563
Here's why.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.119
Imagine inflation as
an airplane descending

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:29.460
to the ground.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.020
When a soft landing occurs,

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.960
that essentially means
the plane, or inflation,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.260
manages to land smoothly on
the ground without any bumps

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.440
or turbulence, which basically
means the Federal Reserve

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.290
is able to bring inflation
down to its target rate,

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.800
which is about 2% without
triggering a negative reaction,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:47.700
like a recession

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.370
or significantly raising
the unemployment rate.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.690
- There's a little bit of a problem

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.190
with a soft landing analogy.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.770
Planes land, economies don't.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.800
The economy is always moving,
it's growing, it's shrinking.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.090
It's not standing still.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.300
So then the question is
if we have a soft landing,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.710
do you just stay there?

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.000
That's hard to do.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.950
- [Narrator] In order to
soft land the economy,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.870
the Fed uses its main tool

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.780
to fight inflation
raising: interest rates,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.930
and the Fed was, until
recently, on a historic run

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.930
of raising interest
rates to a 22-year high

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.160
after a post-pandemic,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.770
strong economic rebound pushed inflation

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.770
to more than 9% last year.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.990
- At today's meeting, the
committee raised the target range

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.457
for the federal funds rate
by 1/4 percentage point,

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:33.810
bringing the target
range to 4 and 3/4 to 5%.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.140
- When you raise interest
rates, it's a blunt instrument.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.550
You don't get to fine tune

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.320
how much you're gonna
slow down the economy.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.410
It isn't like lightly tapping
on the brake on the car,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.410
you stomp on it and things can break.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.820
The concern is that something

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.980
in the economy's going to break.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.660
- [Narrator] The most
commonly referred to example

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.540
of a soft landing
happened in the mid-1990s

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.050
under the leadership of then
Fed Chair Alan Greenspan.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.348
The Fed raised interest
rates rapidly between 1994

00:02:01.348 --> 00:02:04.830
and 1995 from 3% to 6%.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:08.250
Inflation and the unemployment
rate both declined.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.110
- The data that have been published

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.650
in the first weeks of 1995

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.260
have offered some indications

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.790
that the expansion may finally
be slowing from its torrid

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:25.620
and unsustainable pace of late 1994.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.290
- [Narrator] But today's economic
challenges are different.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.850
- Inflation was not a problem

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.330
when the Fed was raising
interest rates in the mid-1990s.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.250
They were doing a preemptive strike.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.800
This is a completely different scenario

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.020
because the Fed has been
trying to do something

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.550
that's never been done before,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.380
which is soft landing the economy

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.830
after a period of much higher inflation.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.500
- [Narrator] Soft landings
are elusive for a reason

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.480
because in order to land the airplane,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.260
you have to avoid a lot of
turbulence, like energy prices,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.940
a hot economy, Fed rate
hikes, and financial crises.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.010
- There are so many
unknowns in the economy.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.960
What's gonna happen to oil prices?

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.700
Are consumers suddenly gonna feel

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.580
like they've gotten their fill
of Taylor Swift and Beyonce

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.410
and they don't need to
spend money anymore?

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.200
They're not so confident
about their income situation.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.840
Jobs are maybe harder to
get and so they pull back.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.240
- [Narrator] One of the primary
threats to a soft landing:

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.710
the Fed itself.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.590
If the Fed holds rates
too tight for too long,

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.663
it could risk a severe downturn.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.590
Inflation has cooled to 3.1%

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.580
and the unemployment rate dropped
to 3.7%, prompting the Fed

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.590
to keep interest rates steady.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.830
- I have always felt since the beginning

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.870
that there was a possibility

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.380
because of the unusual situation

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:47.310
that the economy could cool off in a way

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.700
that enabled inflation to
come down without the kind

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.100
of large job losses that
have often been associated

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.110
with high inflation and tightening cycles.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.910
- They're in wait and see mode.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.650
They think they've probably done enough,

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.900
but they don't wanna declare victory

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.820
or mission accomplished prematurely.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.800
It would be embarrassing
and it could be painful

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.170
for the economy if they
said they were done

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.480
and then they had to go
raise interest rates again.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.800
- [Narrator] While the inflation rate,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:11.880
or the airplane,

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.310
appears to be descending
in the right direction,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.140
it doesn't necessarily mean the Fed

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.210
has soft landed the economy.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:21.120
A lot could change at any given time.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.890
- If we don't have a soft landing,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.800
it's probably because we have a recession.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.900
And then the question
is how bad does it get?

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.400
Is it a mild recession?

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.890
The other side of that is
that we don't have any kind

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.960
of downturn, and in that case,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.150
the Fed would probably get nervous

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.860
if inflation didn't come down enough

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.100
and the economy started to regain speed.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.410
I think the reason soft
landings are so difficult

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.140
to achieve is they just
require the right mix

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.490
of luck and skill.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.980
It's hard to know
without seeing the future

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.888
where exactly you should
set interest rates.

00:04:52.888 --> 00:04:55.555
(lively music)

